    "long_instructions": "Every video got “pan” as its keyword, the most frequent word in each transcript. A good keyword is also rare in the other videos. A term’s tf-idf multiplies its term frequency by its inverse document frequency, the number of videos divided by the number of videos that use the term.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Complete `inverse_document_frequency`. Given a term and the list of videos, it should return the number of videos divided by the number of videos whose tokens include the term.\r\n- Update `score` so that it returns the term’s tf-idf.\r\n- Run the program. Each video should now get a keyword of its own.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nA check such as `\"pan\" in video[\"tokens\"]` tells you whether one video uses a word; your function checks each video for `term`. `len(videos)` is the number of videos. Once your function works, `inverse_document_frequency(\"pan\", videos)` returns `1.0`, because “pan” appears in all three transcripts, and a word found in only one video returns `3.0`.\r\n",
